segment V/56.x), and R7 (via segment Y/67.x) — are in Full state with live
Dead-Time counters, confirming continuous Hello exchange. On R7, the single OSPF
adjacency toward R6 is likewise Full. All FRR subsystem daemons (watchfrr, zebra,
mgmtd, ospfd, staticd) are running on both routers with normal CPU and memory
figures. Routing tables on both routers are completely populated: every prefix in
the OSPF domain is reachable via the expected next-hops. End-to-end data-plane
verification — bidirectional pings between pc0 (20.1.1.10) and pc12 (120.1.1.10),
whose traffic must traverse R6 or R7 — shows 0% packet loss with sub-millisecond
average RTT in both directions. There is no black hole, no routing loop, no
interface fault, and no daemon crash anywhere in the network. The root cause of
the alert is a false premise: no router crashed overnight. No corrective action is
required; the network is functioning exactly as designed and operators can safely
stand down the incident.
